    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d155">

                <pb facs="0280" n="262" xml:id="pg262"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">840.50 Recovery/6–1947: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in France</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Caffery</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">secret</hi>
                        <lb/>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">urgent</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Paris</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1947-06-19">June 19, 1947</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>2440. The British tell me that they have “a firm verbal commitment” from the
                    French that they will go along with them even if the Soviets refuse the
                    invitation to participate in the conversations. They showed me a tentative
                    agenda listing the “form and nature of American aid” in order of importance: <list>
                        <label>(<hi rendition="#italic">a</hi>)</label>
                        <item>Supply of raw materials, foodstuffs and animal feeds.</item>
                        <label>(<hi rendition="#italic">b</hi>)</label>
                        <item>Supply of equipment and means of production.</item>
                        <label>(<hi rendition="#italic">c</hi>)</label>
                        <item>Financial credits.</item>
                    </list>
                </p>
                <p>Duff Cooper<anchor n="1" xml:id="d155-f1"/> expects the Ministers to meet again
                    next week.</p>
                <p>Repeated to London as 459, sent Department 2440.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Caffery</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
